.TH GARNISH\-VM.CONF 5 2026-11-04 garnish "garnish manual"
.SH NAME
garnish\-vm.conf \- configuration of a garnish VM
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I ~/.config/garnish/vms/name.conf
.br
.I ~/.config/garnish/vms/name.conf.d/*.conf
.SH DESCRIPTION
Each file in
.I ~/.config/garnish/vms/
configures one VM for
.BR gerantd (1).
The file name without
.I .conf
is the VM's name, which is also the guest's hostname: letters, digits and
.B \-
(not first or last), at most 32 characters.
.PP
The file is read every time the VM starts, so a change applies at the next
start.
.BR salle (1)
edits these files too, changing only the lines of the settings it writes.
.SH SYNTAX
The format is the one systemd and mkosi use:
.IP \(bu 2
.B [Section]
headers and
.IB Key = Value
settings;
.B #
and
.B ;
start comments.
.IP \(bu
A value continues on the next line if that line is indented or the line
ends with
.BR \e .
.IP \(bu
Assigning a setting again replaces it, except for lists, which grow. An
empty assignment
.RB ( Key= )
resets a setting to its default.
.IP \(bu
Drop-ins in
.I name.conf.d/*.conf
are read after the file, in name order.
.IP \(bu
Relative paths are relative to the file they are in;
.B ~/
is your home directory.
.IP \(bu
List items are separated by spaces; quote an item
.RB ( \(aq...\(aq
or
.BR \(dq...\(dq )
or use
.B \e
to put a space in it.
.IP \(bu
Booleans are
.BR yes / no ,
.BR true / false ,
.BR on / off
or
.BR 1 / 0 .
.IP \(bu
Unknown sections and settings are errors.
.SH [Boot] SECTION
.TP
.BI Image= path
The disk image. Required.
.TP
.BI Kernel= path
The kernel. Default: the image's path with
.I .vmlinuz
in place of its extension.
.TP
.BI Initrd= path
The initrd. Default: the image's path with
.IR .initrd ,
if that file exists.
.TP
.BI KernelCommandLine= string
Replaces the default kernel command line, which is the images':
.EX
root=PARTLABEL=root rw console=ttyS0 systemd.firstboot=off video=Virtual\-1:d
.EE
.TP
.BI KernelCommandLineExtra= words
A list, added to the command line.
.PP
To either,
.B gerantd
adds
.BI systemd.hostname= name\fR,\fP
.B systemd.wants=serial\-getty@ttyS1.service
and
.B systemd.mask=serial\-getty@ttyS0.service
(the login prompt is on the second serial port, for
.BR "gerant console" ),
the guest's address, gateway and DNS servers
.RB ( ip= ,
with
.BR net.ifnames=0 ),
and, with
.BR SharedHome= ,
a
.B systemd.mount\-extra=
for the home directory.
.SH [Machine] SECTION
.TP
.BI Title= text
A name for people, such as
.BR "Debian (work)" .
.BR salle (1)
shows it instead of the VM's name, and the host's sound settings name the
VM's audio stream after it.
.TP
.BI CPUs= n
Default: 4.
.TP
.BI Memory= size
With a
.BR K ,
.BR M ,
.B G
or
.B T
suffix; a plain number is bytes. At least 128M. Default: 4G.
.TP
.BI SharedHome= path
A host directory mounted over the guest user's home with virtio-fs. If the
mount fails, the guest boots with the home from the image.
.TP
.BI Audio= bool
A virtio-snd sound card, through
.BR vhost\-device\-sound .
Default: no.
.TP
.BI Microphone= bool
Lets the guest record from the host's default input. Default: no.
.TP
.BI Wayland= bool
A virtio GPU with
.B GpuContextTypes=
and the host's Wayland socket, which sommelier in the guest uses to show
the guest's windows on the host. Default: yes.
.TP
.BR Clipboard= both | to\-host | from\-host | none
Which way copied data may go:
.B to\-host
lets the host paste what the guest copies, not the other way round;
.B from\-host
is the reverse. Anything but
.B both
runs
.BR videur (1)
between crosvm and the host's Wayland socket. Copy and paste inside the
guest works in every mode. Default: both.
.TP
.BI GpuContextTypes= words
A list. Add
.B virgl2
only if crosvm was built with virglrenderer. Default: cross-domain.
.TP
.BI ExtraArguments= words
A list, passed to
.B crosvm run
as they are.
.SH [Network] SECTION
.TP
.BR Mode= passt | nat | none
.RS
.TP
.B passt
The default. The guest reaches the outside and the host's other addresses
through
.BR passt (1),
which runs as you and makes the guest's connections for it. The guest is
10.0.2.15 with gateway 10.0.2.2, and gets IPv6 when the host has it. The
host can't reach the guest over the network, and nothing needs root.
.TP
.B nat
A tap device of its own on the host, from
.BR gerant\-net (8),
with an address the host can reach and NAT to the outside. The addresses
come from
.BR garnish\-network.conf (5).
.TP
.B none
No network device.
.RE
.IP
In every mode the host reaches the guest over vsock, for
.BR "gerant ssh" ,
the application menu and Wayland. Unless
.B [Pool] DNS=
is set in
.BR gerantd.conf (5),
the guest's DNS queries go to the host's resolver.
.SH [Guest] SECTION
.TP
.BI User= name
The user the image was built with
.RB ( GUEST_USER ),
for the shared home and
.BR "gerant ssh" .
Default: your user name.
.TP
.BI UID= n
.TQ
.BI GID= n
The guest user's IDs, for the shared home. Default: 1000, and the UID.
.TP
.BI AppMenu= bool
Run
.B carte sync
while the VM is up, putting the guest's applications in the host's menu.
With
.B Wayland=yes
and
.BR AppMenu=no ,
.B carte sync \-\-no\-menu
only keeps the list of applications, for
.BR salle (1).
See
.BR carte (1).
Default: yes.
.TP
.BI Notifications= bool
Run
.B sonnette relay
while the VM is up, showing the guest's notifications on the host. See
.BR sonnette (1).
Default: yes.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
# ~/.config/garnish/vms/debian.conf
[Boot]
Image=~/images/debian.raw

[Machine]
Title=Debian
Memory=8G
Audio=yes
Clipboard=to\-host

[Network]
Mode=nat
.EE
.PP
A commented example with every setting is in
.IR /usr/share/doc/gerant/examples/debian.conf .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gerant (1),
.BR gerantd (1),
.BR gerantd.conf (5),
.BR garnish\-network.conf (5),
.BR salle (1)
